# Copy this file to .env and fill in the path. .env is gitignored; this file
# is not, because it is documentation rather than configuration.
#
# Docker Compose reads .env too: compose.yaml mounts this key into the api
# containers, read-only. It is never copied into the image.
#
# The server writes to Firestore and sends notifications as an ADMIN, which
# means a service account rather than a user account. Get the key from the
# Firebase console:
#
#   Project settings > Service accounts > Firebase Admin SDK
#     > Generate new private key
#
# Save the JSON it downloads OUTSIDE any git repo, and put its absolute path
# below. Treat it like a password: it is root access to your Firebase project,
# and it ignores every security rule you write.

GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS=/absolute/path/to/your-service-account.json

# Week 10: on the in-memory steps (0 to 2) the server only knows the four seed
# organisers, which nobody can sign in as. Put your own Firebase user id here
# (the dashboard's "not an organiser" screen shows it) so you can use the
# dashboard on those steps. Several ids: separate them with commas.
# From step 3 the organisers collection in Firestore decides, as in Week 9.
KAI_ORGANISERS=
